sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 5, docs/reference/development-containers.md#nested-fleet): after each Gateway release, on each Node with the nested-fleet switch, the deployment of the nested-fleet Project's default Instance to the release commit. It refreshes the inner fleet and takes the seed that new Instances and tasks of that Project clone. It replaces the release trigger and the daily run of docs/cli/task/vm-image-refresh.mmd. Planned: nothing of this flow is built.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · after each Gateway release
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=default tone=purple
  %% lane F icon=server pill=fleet tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  participant F as Inner fleet<br/>3 containers
  Note over GW: Admit one per Node<br/>release commit
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - each verified Gateway release, from its admission step after Horizon starts; the intent holds the release commit
  %%> When:
  %%> - one deployment per Node with the nested-fleet switch on which the nested-fleet Project's default Instance runs
  %%> - a Node with a clone copy in flight waits until the copy ends: the run replaces the seed that the copy reads
  %%> - a Node with a refresh open keeps it; the newest release commit waits behind it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - best effort: the release admits the run and does not wait for it; a refused admission never fails the release
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  SW->>N: Fetch into the mirror<br/>Ansible · tags fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same fetch as every container default deployment: the Node's mirror, then a bundle into the container; the token never enters it
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts the stopped default first; the inner fleet stays stopped
  %%> - checks out the release commit in `/srv/workspace`
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Project's development deploy steps, as `orbit`: changed dependencies only
  %%> - the image variant's tools change only with a new image, never here
  SW->>F: Start the inner fleet<br/>incus start · wg-nested
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - first places the nested-fleet Swamp Club key from the vault, as a clone does
  %%> - starts the three nested containers and the operator tunnel
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the next steps run through the nested Gateway's own Swamp, which needs the key
  SW->>F: Apply the checkout<br/>nested Gateway · migrate
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the nested Gateway sees the new checkout through its disk device; Orbit migrates and restarts its services
  SW->>F: Converge the nested Nodes<br/>node:converge · app-dev, prod
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the nested Gateway converges app-dev, then app-prod, with the release's agent delivery
  %%> - each must succeed before the next starts
  SW->>F: Wait for Doctor<br/>POST /api/v1/doctor
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same check a proof uses: the nested Gateway is healthy and its three agents are online
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no new `base`, no new seed
  SW->>F: Stop and retake base<br/>incus snapshot · 3 containers
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a snapshot of a running container on a directory pool is a file copy, not a consistent state
  %%> - a restore reverts device and config keys, so `base` is taken after every fleet rule is in place
  SW->>N: Take the seed snapshot<br/>incus stop · seed-COMMIT
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the Club key file, stops the default and snapshots it; ZFS, under 1 s
  %%> - deletes older seeds that no clone uses
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the key is never in `base`, in a seed or in an export
  %%> - the default stays stopped: a stopped container uses no memory, and the next run starts it
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - new Instances and tasks of the Project clone the new seed; existing clones keep the fleet they have
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation names the failed step; the old seed stays the seed, and clones keep copying it
    %%> - the default stays as the run left it; the next release runs again on it
    %%> - open: whether a failure also restores the default to the old seed
  end
